{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Late-horizon upside bias: from ~day 20 onward the model increasingly underpredicts during sustained rallies (true prices accelerate to 72–76 while forecasts stay ~66–71). Apply a positive drift adjustment that grows with horizon when recent trend is up.",
    "Over-mean-reversion after breakouts: following an upside breakout (prices moving from high-60s into 70s), the model pulls forecasts back toward ~68–70 too quickly. Reduce mean-reversion strength when the last 5–10 days show higher highs/higher lows.",
    "Down-move overshoot in mid-horizon: around days ~12–20 the model tends to forecast dips that are slightly too deep/early (predicts mid-64s/65s while realized is a bit higher and turns up sooner). Damp negative deviations in the mid-horizon unless confirmed by a strong recent downswing.",
    "Horizon-dependent calibration: near-term (days 1–10) errors are small and mixed; most systematic error is horizon-amplified. Use minimal correction for days 1–10, moderate for days 11–20, and strongest for days 21–30.",
    "Volatility/convexity miss: in strong uptrends the model underestimates upside convexity (fails to capture late surge). Add a convex trend term (e.g., proportional to recent slope × horizon) when momentum is positive and rising."
  ]
}
